---
import RwLayout from '../../layouts/RwLayout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;

// Rendered as a static checklist. It works as a rubric for the figures, but
// nobody is scored against it; the intro below says so.
const groups = [
  {
    slug: 'wiring',
    title: 'Wiring — which data is this, actually?',
    items: [
      'For each panel, name the exact data series it reads. Use the code or a printed binding at plot time, not the panel’s title.',
      'Compare that name against what the label claims. “Observed” wired to a forecast series is the founding failure of this exercise.',
      'Treat an empty panel as a failure to investigate, never as “no data.” Something asked for a series; find out what it asked for.',
      'Read the console or log output while the figures render. A warning nobody sees is the only symptom some failures produce.',
    ],
  },
  {
    slug: 'units-and-labels',
    title: 'Units and labels',
    items: [
      'Every axis and every legend entry carries a unit.',
      'The unit on the label matches the unit of the data plotted: a label can say °F over °C values and nothing will crash.',
      'Legend entries are display names. Raw internal key names leaking into a legend means the plot is drawing series nobody chose.',
      'The time axis is in UTC and says so. Sanity-check the hours against the location: in Colorado, the afternoon temperature peak lands around 21:00–23:00 UTC. A peak at the wrong hour usually means a timezone error.',
    ],
  },
  {
    slug: 'axes-ranges-scale',
    title: 'Axes, ranges, and scale',
    items: [
      'Axis ranges include the full data without truncating it: a clipped axis quietly deletes the extremes.',
      'Panels meant to be compared share axis ranges; on different scales, the comparison itself misleads.',
      'If a colormap encodes an ordered quantity, it is monotone: no rainbow re-ordering of magnitude.',
    ],
  },
  {
    slug: 'values',
    title: 'Values — the figure against the table',
    items: [
      'Check at least three plotted values against the computed tables, at least one from each figure set.',
      'A metric printing “--”, NaN, or nothing is a finding. In the original episode, the dashes under the wind roses were the only visible sign that the verification had never actually run.',
      'Be suspicious of perfection. A correlation of 1.0 in a verification panel usually means something was compared against itself.',
    ],
  },
];

const sections = [
  { slug: 'wiring', text: 'Wiring' },
  { slug: 'units-and-labels', text: 'Units and labels' },
  { slug: 'axes-ranges-scale', text: 'Axes, ranges, and scale' },
  { slug: 'values', text: 'Values' },
];
---

<RwLayout
  title="Visual checklist — Reference Workflow"
  description="The figure-verification rubric for task 3: wiring, units, axes, and values, each item phrased so it can actually fail."
  current="visual-checklist"
  sections={sections}
>
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900">Visual checklist</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-lg text-gray-600">
      A figure is a claim about the data. This list is where checking the claim starts.
    </p>

    <article class="prose prose-gray max-w-none mt-8">
      <p>
        The verification step of the task-3 prompt has your agent walk this list and report on
        every item. It works like a rubric: each item has a yes-or-no answer backed by evidence,
        because a check that cannot fail checks nothing. Nobody scores you against it.
        The list is a floor, not a ceiling: it names the checks the original episode earned, the
        prompt also has the agent look for problems the list does not name, and passing every
        item does not make a figure correct.
      </p>
    </article>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-8">
      {groups.map(group => (
        <section>
          <h2 id={group.slug} class="text-xl font-semibold text-gray-900">{group.title}</h2>
          <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2">
            {group.items.map(item => (
              <li class="flex gap-3 rounded-lg border border-gray-200 bg-gray-50/60 px-4 py-3">
                <span class="mt-0.5 select-none text-gray-400" aria-hidden="true">☐</span>
                <span class="text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-gray-700">{item}</span>
              </li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </section>
      ))}
    </div>

    <article class="prose prose-gray max-w-none mt-10">
      <p>
        Back to <a href={`${base}reference-workflow/task-3/`}>task 3</a>, or the
        <a href={`${base}reference-workflow/statistics/`}>statistics appendix</a> for what the
        plotted quantities mean.
      </p>
    </article>
</RwLayout>
